  message: "2026-06-10 design-interview ruling (ADR-0038, merged in PR #4181): access_mode is rebuilt per-edge from the AST role rather than inherited from emitter defaults, with a validator rule that ast_name_read implies access_mode != write; the 'mutate' cell is kept. Stays violated until the per-edge rebuild lands; re-validate against the new validator rule."  # d546
- op: discuss  # 47de
  at: '2026-06-28T12:30:37Z'  # 47de
  by: agent  # 47de
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 47de
  clock: 8  # 47de
  nonce: 47de  # 47de
  message: "Scoping investigation (2026-06-28, autonomous): deferring this for human scope input — it is meatier than it reads and has a judgment component. Findings:\n- The read-evidence-type creation sites do NOT set access_mode at Edge.create time (e.g. the ast_name_read `references` edge at py.py:3020 passes no access_mode kwarg; Edge.create only stores access_mode when non-None). So the bogus access_mode='write' on `references`/`module_attr_ref` edges is applied DOWNSTREAM, not at creation — the mechanism needs tracing (annotate_dataflow_ast in core/dataflow.py only runs on call_edges per py.py:4225, so the source for non-call edges is not yet pinned). This is a multi-site/structural fix, not a one-liner.\n- Symptoms 1 & 2 (read-evidence-types ast_name_read / function_reference / module_attribute_reference must yield access_mode='read', not 'write') are UNAMBIGUOUS and high-value (≈49% of `references` edges + ≈95% of `module_attr_ref` carry a self-contradiction). The clean fix is the same \"derive from evidence_type\" pattern as confidence:F1.\n- Symptom 3 (collection-mutator calls append/add/extend/setdefault/write → 'mutate' not 'write') is a VOCABULARY-JUDGMENT call: which method names count as mutators, and per which receiver type (list.append mutates; str has no append) — the same denylist-asymptote problem as io_boundary. This part wants a human ruling on scope (heuristic name-list vs receiver-typed).\nProposed split when picked up: fix 1&2 structurally (derive access_mode from evidence_type at the downstream annotator, once the non-call-edge site is pinned), and file symptom 3 as a separate sub-item with the mutator-vocabulary decision. Not started; left violated."  # 47de
- op: discuss  # 1bcd
  at: '2026-06-28T13:54:38Z'  # 1bcd
  by: agent  # 1bcd
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 1bcd
  clock: 9  # 1bcd
  nonce: 1bcd  # 1bcd
  message: "SYMPTOMS 1 & 2 FIXED (PR merged to selfh, commit e70e2ef759) — item stays `violated` pending symptom 3 (the mutator-vocabulary decision). Behavioral evidence, fresh `hypergumbo run packages/hypergumbo-core/src` on the post-fix editable install:\n  - Symptom 1 (references edges with evidence_type ast_name_read / function_reference wrongly access_mode='write'): contradiction count 0 (1034 read-evidence references edges, ALL now access_mode='read'). WAS ~2082 / 49% of references edges self-contradictory.\n  - Symptom 2 (module_attr_ref edges with module_attribute_reference wrongly 'write'): contradiction count 0 (329 module_attr_ref edges, ALL now 'read'). WAS ~531 / 95% of populated module_attr_ref.\nRoot cause + fix: annotate_dataflow_ast (core/dataflow.py) derived access_mode from the line's statement kind (ast.Assign line -> 'write'), with a downgrade-to-'read' that only covered edge_type=='calls'. So a read sitting on an assignment line (the RHS) inherited 'write'. Fix: read-evidence-type edges (new _READ_EVIDENCE_TYPES = {ast_name_read, function_reference, module_attribute_reference}) now short-circuit to access_mode='read' BEFORE the line-statement heuristic — deriving from evidence type, the invariant's stated principle. Scope note: this corrects the mislabel on annotated (assignment/for/with) lines; a read-evidence edge on a line with no statement-mode and no library-pattern match still gets no access_mode (None) — that is the existing no-annotation case, not a 'write' mislabel, so out of scope for symptoms 1&2.\n\nREMAINING — symptom 3 (still violated): collection-mutator calls (.append/.add/.extend/.setdefault, top callees on self) carry access_mode='write' rather than 'mutate'. This is the VOCABULARY-JUDGMENT call flagged in the 2026-06-28 scoping note: which method names count as mutators, and whether receiver type matters (list.append mutates; str has no append; .write to a file is arguably a true 'write' not 'mutate'). The current python.yaml library_patterns deliberately maps .append/.write/.send -> write. Needs a human ruling on the mutator method-set (and the write-vs-mutate boundary for .write) before implementation. Keeping INV-kudug `violated` until symptom 3 lands."  # 1bcd
- op: discuss  # ea30
  at: '2026-06-28T14:29:21Z'  # ea30
  by: agent  # ea30
  actor: jgstern_agent  # ea30
  clock: 10  # ea30
  nonce: ea30  # ea30
  message: "SYMPTOM 3 — concrete proposal for human ruling (drafted 2026-06-28; read-only design-prep, nothing implemented). The fix is a small edit to python.yaml `library_patterns`; the only blocker is the vocabulary decision. Current production mapping (dataflow_patterns/python.yaml) and proposed changes:\n\nPROPOSE write -> mutate (in-place collection modification; the item's \"collection mutations labeled mutate\"):\n  - .append   (1183 self-corpus calls)   write -> mutate\n  - .add      (282)                       write -> mutate\n  - .extend   (151)                       write -> mutate\n  - .insert                               write -> mutate\nPROPOSE add (currently NO pattern -> falls through to line-mode/None):\n  - .setdefault (86)                      (new) -> mutate\nKEEP as-is (already correct):\n  - .update                               mutate  (already)\n  - .pop / .remove / .clear / .discard / .popitem   delete  (the 4-cell `delete` is the more specific value for element removal)\nKEEP as write (NOT mutate) — the write-vs-mutate boundary:\n  - .write (63) / .writelines / .send / .sendall / .put / .put_nowait\n    These write to an EXTERNAL SINK (file / socket / queue), not an in-place collection mutation. The item's symptom-3 list lumped .write(63) in, but a file write IS a write — recommend it stays `write`. (Consistent with the item's own note that event_publishes edges \"ARE writes\".)\n\nTHREE DECISIONS NEEDED:\n  (A) Approve .append/.add/.extend/.insert/.setdefault -> mutate?  [recommend YES]\n  (B) Keep .write/.send/.put as write (external-sink writes), NOT mutate?  [recommend YES]\n  (C) Keep delete-family as `delete`, or recategorize to `mutate`?  [recommend KEEP delete]\n\nCAVEAT (the denylist-asymptote): these match by METHOD NAME regardless of receiver type (python.yaml's stated tradeoff: \"only methods whose name unambiguously implies the access mode regardless of receiver\"). .append/.add overwhelmingly mean list/set mutation; a custom non-collection .append would be mislabeled mutate (cost: a slightly-wrong access_mode, not a crash). This proposal re-labels 4 existing name-patterns + adds 1; it does not change the name-based approach already in use, so it doesn't worsen the asymptote.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION once ruled (est. 1 small PR): edit python.yaml (5 patterns), add a test asserting .append -> mutate through the real python.yaml (the existing .append tests use synthetic configs, so none break), CHANGELOG, close INV-kudug `satisfied` with a before->after mutate-count on self-analysis. Symptoms 1&2 already fixed (e70e2ef759); this is the last piece."  # ea30
